00:00:04.309 --> 00:00:06.740 align:start position:0%
this is a brine shrimp as it moves  through the water it creates swirling  Eddie's behind it a single shrimp only  moves a tiny amount of water but in a  large group could creatures like these  move enough water to influence the  mixing of an ocean  to find out research has kept groups of  brine shrimp in tall tanks in their lab  then they suspended tiny particles in  the water which they could track to  analyze its flow by shining a light from  the top of the tank they could cause the  swarm to swim up mimicking the natural  migratory behavior of other shrimp like  krill and as they rose something  surprising happened  together the shrimp produced a jet of  water as large as the swarm itself  forcing water down as they swam up a  decade ago scientists didn't think this  kind of aggregation effect was possible  but this research shows that it can  happen and according to the researchers  on a large enough scale to impact the  climate you see brine shrimp are only a  model organism but the researchers think  that this phenomenon might also happen  with critters like krill which live in  climatically sensitive areas like the  southern ocean  water jets on the scale of swarms of  krill which can get large enough to see  from space could have major impacts on  ocean mixing changing the balance of  ocean chemistry nutrient flows and even  the climate now this effect has only  been seen in a lab and predictions about  its impacts are mostly speculation but  if confirmed in field studies it could  represent an entirely new role played by  some of the most abundant animals on the  planet not to mention oceanographers  would have a brand new variable to add  to their models  [Music]
 

